A||||7||speed||Cylinder head swap
Z||000000||speed||03-30-99||08:54 PM||||I would like to know if a 3.4 cylinder head will fit the 2.8 short block with out modifications. If they fit I am planing to install these heads on a 3.1 block ( sames as 2.8 but stroker) and put a turbo charger in. I also would like to know if any intercooler has been installed on a Fiero v6 and in part of the car.
Z||000001||theogre||03-31-99||05:59 PM||||Couldn't say for sure, but I don't think that head will fit.<p>why don't you just port & polish the original head. that will do as much, or more, than just sticking bigger valves on top.<p>W/o an over roof air scoop.... I doubt you'll get enough air to make an intercooler any use. fitting it into the bay will be a real headache.<p>With the engine, and engine compartment, laid out like they are,,, did you look into a centrifical blower? would take up less space, doesn't preheat the air charge as baddly, and doesn't need nearly as much plumbing. 
Z||000002||speed||03-31-99||09:14 PM||||Yes I will port and polish the runners and also try to fit bigger valves if available.<br>On the centrifugal super charger I have not seen this job done. I have my own machine shop and I can make my own parts but the super chargers are very expensive.I can purshase a new turbo and all the parts for less money(that is what I have seen up to now). Have you seen a super charger installed in  Fiero? I have an idea of instalation but I will have to build a shaft to install it a the left side of the motor( above the transaxle). Please let me know if you have a better option.<br>Thanks
Z||000003||theogre||04-02-99||09:40 AM||||well,,,, depends some on what year the motor is.... if it's a pre 87 motor, things are diferant then 87/88 which use a sepent belt.<p>Looking at my own 87, I'd say if you don't have AC, then the blower could be mounted where the AC compressor goes. you have to fool with brakets, but the pullys are already  setup, you just need the right belt.<p>Most C blowers i've seen.... run on a serpent belt. they add a crank pully if need be to do this. For the giggles of it... look up paxton, or vortech, and see if they have a kit. <p>C blowers generally don't want/need allot of engine changes beyond port,polish. Even turbo's generally don't need bigger vavles. Niether want anything done that would raise compression. (if you are going over 5-7PSI boost you usually want to drop engine compression from stock.)<p>mounting on the back side, feeding under the alternator, you would need a drive shaft, and an idler pully, to maintain belt contact area on the alternator and blower drive. At least on the 87.<p>on the pre 87, if you add the crank pully, and run the blower on it's own belt, you probably are going to have a clearance problem with the strut tower. The alternator just clears as it is. If you run off the alternator belt you again need an idler, and I think you'll have problems with keeping V belts from breaking. (serpents are stronger, that's why the blower makers upgrade the crank pullys if need be to cary one.)<p>You are probably right that the turbo is easier to hook up. there is plenty of room over the trany, tho I'd make a heat shield for between the trubo & tranny. you could retain the stock exhaust. but you need to make a new manifold. You can use the flange from an old stock manifold to connect to the stock exhuast. This would make future repaires easier.<p>If your dealling with a used turbo, and rebiulding it, then you might want to find one from a Ford turbo mustang or T bird. Those are 2.3 litre motors. I've seen them put on 2.8 motors with good results, so they should be fine for 2.5's as well.<p>Unless you're also rebuilding the botom end compleatly, I'd be worried about the crank, and rods, breaking. stock 2.5's aren't the strongest motors on the block. I don't think I'd put more than 5 PSI boost on it w/o major upgrades.
Z||000004||theogre||04-02-99||09:48 AM||||For turbo, any turbo, you also want to install an Oil Acumulator. This device feeds oil to the turbo after engine shut down to prevent bearing failure.<p>For any readers that have a factory turbo charged car, you probably don't have this. (this is why the manufacturor tells you to Idle the car a few minutes before shut off.)You can add this device youself. they are available from most speed shops.<p>wihtout the acumulator, your turbo life will likely be shortened considerably. Most people don't follow the idle before shutoff rule, and that means the spinning turbo has no oil flow while is spins down.
Z||000005||speed||04-02-99||04:29 PM||||I got an email that indicates that the 3.4 Camaro motor looks like it has enough meat to hold a turbo. My car is an 87 V6 GT that has serpant. Do you know if the trans on my motor will fit the 3.4 without having to spend to much time on adapters. Is their any kits in the market for upgrading the auto trans that came with the car.<br>I saw a Pontiac Gran Am turbo and it looked like it had 16" alloy wheels similar to the original style that came with the 87 GT (15") do know if they fit the Fiero.<br>I am asking all these questions because I just bought the Fiero and have no knowledge at all on these cars. I had 88 Corvette in which I installed a supercharger. I sold it and now I have my new toy(which I like).<br>Thanks
Z||000006||raceknb||04-02-99||09:54 PM||||I don't recall any of the grand am's having a turbo.  The sunbird had the turbo option back in the mid 80's.  They only ran on 15s.  The 16" wheel was still for the rich man, only coming on corvette and camero and such.  Not to say that Chevy didn't make a grand am turbo, just never saw one in this neck of the woods.  and if they did...It would have been the 2.0 turbo like the sunbird which only had a t1.5 turbine on it.<br>NO BOOST AT ALL.  Could outrun it with a 3 wheeled Yugo...
